Randomized controlled trials assess ketamine’s effectiveness in neuropathic pain mainly by comparing ketamine with placebo or standard treatment under blinded randomization, then measuring changes in patient-reported pain and side effects.

More specifically, the RCT evidence described in the retrieved sources shows that:
- trials are commonly <cite id="4390f033-6">randomized, doubleblind, crossover, placebo-controlled</cite>;
- effectiveness is judged by whether ketamine produces a <cite id="4390f033-6">improvement in daily pain intensity</cite> or broader pain reduction;
- pooled RCT evidence asks whether ketamine added to usual care leads to better outcomes, and one meta-analysis found <cite id="4390f033-0">a statistically significant pain reduction by adding ketamine to the treatment of chronic NP when compared to the standard treatment</cite>;
- some reviews of ketamine infusion trials also compare against placebo and summarize efficacy by estimated effect size, reporting <cite id="4390f033-3">effect compared with placebo</cite> and that <cite id="4390f033-3">IV Ketamine infusion is efficacious and safe in patients with chronic pain</cite>;
- however, RCTs also assess harms, because ketamine’s value must be weighed against <cite id="4390f033-5">unpleasant adverse effects, and long-term safety issues</cite>, and some summaries report <cite id="4390f033-8">an increased risk of adverse effects such as delusions</cite>.

So, in practice, RCTs test ketamine by randomizing neuropathic pain patients to ketamine or control, blinding treatment assignment, tracking pain-intensity outcomes over time, and recording adverse events. The overall evidence suggests some trials and meta-analyses show benefit, but results are mixed and safety monitoring is a key part of the assessment. <cite id="4390f033-0">There is a statistically significant pain reduction by adding ketamine to the treatment of chronic NP when compared to the standard treatment.</cite> <cite id="4390f033-6">A randomized, doubleblind, crossover, placebo-controlled study ... showed no improvement in daily pain intensity.</cite>
